# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Facility Issue Tracker — configuration
#
#   cp .env.example .env   and fill in the REQUIRED block below.
#
# Everything after it has a working default. Then `./deploy.sh`, and
# `./scripts/link-local.sh > .env.local` to run the UI locally against the
# cluster you just deployed.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ══ REQUIRED — deploy.sh refuses to run without these ═══════════════════════

# Your DigitalOcean API token, read/write. Used by the deploy scripts only —
# never given to the deployed app or to an agent.
DIGITALOCEAN_ACCESS_TOKEN=

# A DigitalOcean *model access key* — not the API token above. deploy.sh
# refuses to run without one, and refuses one equal to the API token.
#
#   https://cloud.digitalocean.com/gen-ai/model-access-keys
#
# This key is handed to the intake agent as a secret, and that agent puts
# anonymous complaint text straight into its prompt while holding a shell and
# unrestricted egress. A model access key buys inference if it leaks. An
# account token buys the account.
INFERENCE_API_KEY=

# Password for the /admin dashboard. Change it — deploy.sh warns if you don't.
ADMIN_PASSWORD=complaints

# Your fork of this repo, as owner/repo — App Platform builds from it. The
# DigitalOcean GitHub app must be able to read it.
GITHUB_REPO=

# ══ Optional — everything below has a working default ═══════════════════════

# ── GitHub issue tracker (optional, off by default) ─────────────────────────
# Off: the demo is the harness alone — every complaint becomes a ticket on the
# wall, and GitHub is never touched. Nothing below needs filling in.
#
# On: each ticket also opens a labelled issue in GITHUB_ISSUE_REPO, through
# Action Gateway. There is no token for this on purpose: Action Gateway holds
# the GitHub credential and substitutes it when the tool runs, so it never
# reaches the sandbox. After deploying, authorize it once:
#
#   ./scripts/connect-github.sh
#
# Turning it on or off takes effect on the next ./deploy.sh.
ENABLE_GITHUB_ISSUES=false

# Required when ENABLE_GITHUB_ISSUES=true. A repo you own, as owner/repo,
# used only as an issue tracker — every ticket becomes an issue there, and
# Reset deletes them all.
GITHUB_ISSUE_REPO=

# Read by scripts/connect-github.sh only.
#
# AG_SCOPES: comma-separated GitHub OAuth scopes. The default is what the
# issue and label tools declare; `repo` alone files issues but cannot label
# them. `default` asks for the platform's nine, including workflow and gist.
AG_SCOPES=repo,read:user

# ── Model ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Used by the agents only — every model call happens in an agent sandbox,
# through DigitalOcean's inference service. The web app never gets these.
INFERENCE_BASE_URL=https://inference.do-ai.run

# The DigitalOcean model slug of your preference. List what is available:
#   doctl serverless-inference models list
INFERENCE_MODEL=qwen3.8-max

# ── Deploy settings (read by deploy.sh / destroy.sh, not by the app) ────────

GITHUB_BRANCH=main

# Name prefix for every resource deploy.sh creates. Also what destroy.sh
# matches on, and what you type to confirm a teardown. Lowercase letters,
# numbers and hyphens only — it becomes the app and database names, which
# reject underscores.
STACK_NAME=facility-tracker-demo

# Datacentre for the app and the database. Must support Managed Postgres:
#   doctl databases options regions --engine pg
DO_REGION=nyc3

# How many intake triggers to create. A Harness Runtime trigger runs exactly
# one session at a time, so this is how many complaints can be processed at
# once — one trigger means the nth person waits about n minutes. A trigger
# does nothing until it is fired. Verified working at 8.
INTAKE_SHARDS=8

# Sizes. These are the smallest that comfortably run the demo.
#   basic-xxs        512MB   the web tier does almost nothing
#   db-s-1vcpu-1gb   1GB     smallest single-node Postgres
APP_SIZE=basic-xxs
DB_SIZE=db-s-1vcpu-1gb

# ── Local development ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PORT=3000
NODE_ENV=development

# Signs the admin session cookie when running locally. deploy.sh generates
# its own for App Platform, so this never reaches production.
# Generate: openssl rand -hex 32
SESSION_SECRET=dev-only-change-me

# ── Database ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# DigitalOcean Managed Postgres, in every environment — there is no local
# database. deploy.sh binds this automatically on App Platform.
#
# To run the UI locally against the deployed cluster, don't set this by hand:
#
#   ./scripts/link-local.sh > .env.local
#
# which writes DATABASE_URL and the cluster CA into .env.local (it overrides
# .env). Until you have deployed once, there is nothing to point at.
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:pass@host:25060/complaints?sslmode=require

# ── Harness Runtime ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every complaint fires this webhook, and a fresh microVM writes the ticket.
# There is no in-process alternative: the agent's instructions live in
# agents/complaint-prompt.txt and that is the only copy of them.
#
# Written for you by `./scripts/link-local.sh > .env.local` after a deploy.
# MARS_WEBHOOK_URL=
# MARS_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# MARS_TICKET_TABLE=tickets

# ── Reset, and the one credential the demo does not use ─────────────────────
#
# Only used when ENABLE_GITHUB_ISSUES=true. Optional even then: leave it empty
# and everything works; Reset just leaves the tracker alone and you clear it
# by hand.
#
# Why it exists, stated plainly because a token in a config file deserves a
# reason. Everything the *demo* does with GitHub goes through Action Gateway,
# which holds the credential and substitutes it when a tool runs, so no token
# ever reaches a sandbox. That is the thing being demonstrated.
#
# Reset is not the demo. It is the button you press between rehearsals, and it
# needs one capability the demo deliberately lacks: delete. Action Gateway
# exposes update_issue and no delete — correct for an agent that should not be
# able to erase its own audit trail — so without this the tracker fills up with
# hundreds of closed issues over a conference day.
#
# Scope it tightly:
#   - a fine-grained PAT, this ONE repository, nothing else
#   - Issues: read & write, plus Metadata: read
#   - it needs admin on that repo, because GitHub's deleteIssue is a GraphQL
#     mutation that requires it; there is no REST delete and no lesser
#     permission that works
#   - give it an expiry and rotate it
#
# It is a SECRET in the App Platform spec, it is never passed to an agent, a
# trigger or a sandbox, and it is only reachable from POST /api/reset behind
# the admin password. See src/lib/github-admin.js.
GITHUB_ISSUE_TOKEN=
